    "text": "96\n\n Why Your Letters of Intent Are Being Ignored\nHi Sara,\nSubmitting letters of intent (LOIs) but getting crickets in response? You\u2019re not alone.\nI\u2019ve talked to hundreds of buyers in the accounting industry, and there\u2019s one big reason LOIs often end up in the seller\u2019s trash bin: you\u2019re not answering the one question sellers care about most.\n\u201cHow Are You Going to Take Care of My Business?\u201d\nIt seems obvious, but for sellers\u2014especially those running CPA firms\u2014it\u2019s the dealbreaker. If your LOI doesn\u2019t explain how you\u2019ll manage their highly regulated and sophisticated practice, especially if you\u2019re not a CPA yourself, you\u2019re unlikely to get a call back.\nHere\u2019s why:\nCPA-Specific Work Matters:\nNot all accounting is created equal. While bookkeeping, payroll, and general tax prep fall under \u201caccounting,\u201d CPA-specific tasks\u2014like audit and attestation work\u2014require that coveted CPA designation.\n\nRegulatory Hurdles Exist:\nIn most jurisdictions, CPA firms must be majority-owned by CPAs (51% or more). In some states, like South Carolina, the threshold is even higher at 66.6%. Without a CPA designation, you can\u2019t own these businesses outright.\n\nSellers Care About Their Legacy:\nSellers work hard to build their practices. They\u2019re not just selling a business; they\u2019re entrusting their clients to you and sometimes their employees as well. If your plan doesn\u2019t inspire confidence, they\u2019ll pass.\n\nHow to Fix Your LOI\nHere\u2019s how to address sellers\u2019 concerns upfront:\nHave a Clear Ownership Plan:\nIf you\u2019re not a CPA, acknowledge the regulatory requirements and provide a solution that answers their question before they can ask it. \nOptions include:\nPartnering with a CPA to co-own the firm.\n\nStructuring the deal as a general accounting practice (if audit work isn\u2019t involved).\n\nUsing an alternative practice structure (APS) to spin off CPA-specific tasks into a separate CPA-owned entity.\n\nDon\u2019t Default to \u201cI\u2019ll Hire Someone\u201d:\nHiring a tax manager or offshoring CPA-specific tasks might sound plenty fine to you, but sellers are understandably very skeptical of this pie in the sky solution. Show you\u2019ve done the homework by (at the very least) identifying or partnering with qualified talent. Even still, you should know that some sellers are simply not going to consider a non-CPA buyer.\n\nPrioritize Client Care:\nSellers want assurance that their clients will be taken care of. Your LOI should show empathy for their concerns and confidence in your ability to maintain those relationships.\n\nAt the end of the day, sellers aren\u2019t just chasing the highest offer. They want a buyer who understands the industry, respects their hard work, and has a realistic plan for the future.\nIf you\u2019re struggling to craft LOIs that stand out, check out our Become the Expert: Letter of Intent Course right here.\u00a0\nA little preparation can go a long way in getting sellers to say, \u201cLet\u2019s move forward.\u201d\nHere\u2019s to Smarter Transitions,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nBusiness Consultant and Founder of Deal Academy\n\n For more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2025\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
